WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.283
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.283 --> 00:00:02.700
JEFFREY GROSSMAN:
So we've sort of

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.850
got into this place where
we can make anything

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.340
we want all the way to scale
the atom while at the same time,

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.790
we need that more than ever
to solve the most pressing

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:12.930
global challenges.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:15.540
It's a really exciting
time to understand

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.320
how atoms come together and
what we can do with that.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.770
SARAH HANSEN: Today on Chalk
Radio, we're talking atoms.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:24.570
In this episode,
we're going to see

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:28.410
what it takes to bring
concepts from theory to reality

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.630
and how one professor
at MIT has learned

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:33.930
to make his course
content real, tangible,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.630
and important to his students.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.540
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: If
you can touch and feel

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.280
some aspect of what
you're learning,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.380
then you learn it
in a richer way.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.690
You learn it from
a different angle.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:50.680
You see the problem a
little bit differently.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.180
SARAH HANSEN: We're
going to explore

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.910
the world of solid
state chemistry

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:59.440
course 3.091, which as
always is available on MIT

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.415
OpenCourseWare.

00:01:00.415 --> 00:01:03.940
And to take us through, we're
talking with Professor Jeffrey

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.027
Grossman.

00:01:05.027 --> 00:01:07.610
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: I am the head
of the Department of Materials

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:09.910
Science and Engineering at MIT.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:14.470
I teach 3.091, and I do
it as the lead instructor.

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:17.320
But it's only possible
to teach it effectively

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:20.740
because I have an
outstanding staff of TAs

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.830
as well as a wonderful
course administrator who

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:24.790
all come together.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:27.830
And we give it our
all every fall.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.212
So that's why the course works.

00:01:30.212 --> 00:01:32.170
SARAH HANSEN: And this
course, like many others

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:34.180
we've highlighted
on our show, needs

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.640
a team that understands
how to speak and appeal

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.150
to a general audience, an
audience that may not know much

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.280
about chemistry to begin with.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.750
This course is what we at MIT
call a GIR or General Institute

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.740
Requirement.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:52.183
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: Before a
student can graduate from MIT

00:01:52.183 --> 00:01:53.600
with an undergraduate
degree, they

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.150
have to have taken one
semester of chemistry.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:00.710
So we emphasize the basic
building blocks of chemistry,

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.630
and then we build
solids out of those.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:05.630
And so the basic building blocks
are atoms and the way atoms

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:09.229
bond together and then how
those atoms come together

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:10.550
to make solid materials.

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:13.070
And that's why the
course that I teach

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.930
is called solid state chemistry.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.150
SARAH HANSEN: You've
probably heard of the Stone

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:22.160
Age and the Iron Age.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:24.950
But what age are
we living in now?

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.590
Part of what makes this
course so interesting

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.200
and one of the reasons that
I wanted to share it with you

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.600
is that it invites you
to explore questions just

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.840
like this, questions that
take a contemporary look

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.895
at solid state chemistry.

00:02:37.895 --> 00:02:39.770
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: So as
a materials scientist

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:44.300
and engineer, I love
the fact that we

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:49.070
tend to name the age we live
in by the sort of material that

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:51.080
mattered most in that age.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.560
I would say that recently,
we've had the age of silicon,

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:57.140
the age of plastics.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:03.170
I think that we have reached a
time in science and engineering

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:07.760
where we will never be
able to do that again i.e.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.750
to name the age we live in
by some single material.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.390
And the reason is that
we are now at a place

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:18.080
where we can actually
design any material any way

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.960
we want all the way down to
the atoms that make it up.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.900
And so that's a kind of new era.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.750
And I call that the age
of materials design.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:31.590
It's really the age of atomic
scale materials design.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.980
There's a very famous
speech that Richard Feynman

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.280
gave called "There's Plenty
of Room at the Bottom."

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.020
And it was really
the first vision

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:41.810
of what would happen
if you could move atoms

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.450
around anywhere you wanted.

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.570
And, well, you could make
pretty much anything.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:51.300
And so the question then
is how could you do that?

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.760
And then the other question
is what would you make?

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.390
SARAH HANSEN: And if that wasn't
enough to pique your interest,

00:03:57.390 --> 00:04:00.510
Professor Grossman makes
sure to apply these questions

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:05.093
to the issues that affect
humanity on a global scale.

00:04:05.093 --> 00:04:07.260
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: What is
so exciting about the fact

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.270
that we now are
able to put atoms

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:13.790
anywhere we want, that has
come at a critical moment

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.890
in the challenge
that we face globally

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.950
in energy and climate
and sustainability

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:21.390
and many other challenges.

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:25.140
Because at the heart of
getting through and solving

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:30.270
these really hard problems lies
the design of a new material.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.420
And so we've gotten
to this place where

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.090
we can make anything we want
all the way to scale the atom

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:39.480
while at the same time, we
need that more than ever

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.210
to solve the most pressing
global challenges.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.102
It's a really exciting
time to understand

00:04:45.102 --> 00:04:47.310
how atoms come together and
what we can do with that.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:51.720
SARAH HANSEN: To
Professor Grossman,

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.840
the real world applications
of solid state chemistry

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.450
are just as important as
the theoretical concepts

00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:01.980
so much so in fact that every
single lecture has an added

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.740
element that explains how
students can practically apply

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.853
the concepts in the real world.

00:05:06.853 --> 00:05:08.270
JEFFREY GROSSMAN:
Just because I'm

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.910
teaching say a lecture
on the periodic table,

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:14.150
or I'm teaching a lecture
on Lewis structures,

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:17.450
or I'm teaching a lecture
on X-ray generation,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:21.530
it doesn't mean I can't connect
in each case to some sort

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:24.620
of bigger picture
question or something

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:26.780
that they can really
connect to that

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.260
is more broadly accessible.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.530
I call it my "Why this
Matters" moment because it

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.450
gives context.

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:38.960
And I think we as
teachers should

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.700
be able to do that with
anything we're teaching.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.010
So for every single one of
the 36 lectures that I teach,

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.530
I take a "Why this
Matters" moment.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:51.500
And I connect what it is
that we learned right there

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:56.780
in that lecture to some sort
of pressing global challenge,

00:05:56.780 --> 00:05:58.820
or maybe it's a
technology that they all

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:02.680
know or can connect to
just to give that context.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.430
SARAH HANSEN: And as
you may have guessed,

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.560
the "Why this
Matters" moments are

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:09.200
a favorite part of Professor
Grossman's lectures

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.975
and not just for the students.

00:06:10.975 --> 00:06:13.100
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: It's also
really important for me

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.540
because it makes me excited.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.210
And it makes me
every year come up

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:21.110
with new ideas for how to relate
the material to some bigger

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:23.870
picture aspect of their lives.

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.290
In the first year, I
actually wound up sometimes

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:28.645
cutting that short.

00:06:28.645 --> 00:06:30.020
Sometimes I had
too much material

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.200
to get through in a lecture.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.010
And so we didn't get to
the "Why this Matters."

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:36.320
And what I heard
from students was

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.440
how that was such an
important part of each lecture

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:41.390
that they didn't want
me to ever cut it short.

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:45.350
And so I've been very protective
of those moments of class.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.140
Because as soon as I put
the "Why this Matters"

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.430
moment in front of them, they
sit at the edge of their seats.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.670
I feel that this is
something that we

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:58.190
could do in any class we teach.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:02.330
I don't care if it's some
advanced algebra or a Python

00:07:02.330 --> 00:07:04.730
programming class or
civil engineering.

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.000
It doesn't matter.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.442
Every time we teach,
I think that we

00:07:08.442 --> 00:07:09.650
can take what we're teaching.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:14.030
And we can try to give
it some sort of context,

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:18.112
how what they've just learned
connects to something else.

00:07:18.112 --> 00:07:20.570
SARAH HANSEN: At the heart of
Professor Grossman's teaching

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:23.940
is a focus on allowing his
students to see, touch,

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.060
and interact with
the subject matter,

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.490
to make it apparent
to the senses in order

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:30.210
to make it matter.

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:35.690
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: I always feel
that if you can touch and feel

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:37.880
some aspect of what
you're learning, then

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.520
you learn it in a richer way.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:42.840
You learn it from
a different angle.

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:46.500
You see the problem a
little bit differently.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.752
So in the very first
lecture, in fact,

00:07:48.752 --> 00:07:50.210
in the introductory
lecture, I talk

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:53.270
about how it is that
chemistry came to be.

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:55.700
What was the early
goals of chemists?

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:56.940
What were they trying to do?

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:58.398
And one of the
things that they did

00:07:58.398 --> 00:08:01.655
is they took things like
a rock and say, well,

00:08:01.655 --> 00:08:02.780
how do you break this down?

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:03.980
What's it made of?

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:07.760
And so they smash it, or
they light stuff on fire.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:10.070
We don't tell our students
to do that, by the way.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:13.670
Or they mix things
together to smash it.

00:08:13.670 --> 00:08:14.660
They break it.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:15.680
They mix it with that.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:16.790
They mix it with that.

00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:18.500
And they say,
well, what happens?

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:22.370
And through that came
discovery of the basic building

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:23.853
blocks of matter.

00:08:23.853 --> 00:08:25.520
And so what I wanted
is I wanted to say,

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:29.870
well, OK, let's put ourselves
back in that position.

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:31.700
At the time, my
youngest daughter was 6.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:34.880
And so she had had something
like three birthday parties

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.010
she went to in one weekend.

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:40.580
And so our hallway had a whole
bunch of these goody bags

00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:44.660
with candy and Disney toys.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:47.600
And I'd look at those and think
maybe we could put chemistry

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:48.740
in these bags.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:53.100
So we created a set of goody
bags, about one each week.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:54.560
And so in the very
first goody bag,

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.857
I give students five
different strips of something.

00:08:59.857 --> 00:09:01.190
I don't tell them what they are.

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:04.460
And I give them a ruler
to measure the strips.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:05.900
They can weigh them.

00:09:05.900 --> 00:09:07.450
And I give them some vinegar.

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:10.748
And again, the idea is to try
to put them in the position of,

00:09:10.748 --> 00:09:12.290
well, let me give
you things that you

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:13.332
don't know what they are.

00:09:13.332 --> 00:09:14.900
How would you figure that out?

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:16.640
And then gets to a
really important part

00:09:16.640 --> 00:09:19.070
of the goody bag, it
is meant to complement

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:22.010
what I'm teaching by
giving a way for them

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:25.200
to touch and hold and
interact with the chemistry,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.590
but it's also meant
for them if they

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:29.840
want to go beyond
what we're learning

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.877
and to try their own ideas out.

00:09:32.877 --> 00:09:34.460
SARAH HANSEN: Professor
Grossman knows

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:36.950
his methods aren't
exactly conventional

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:40.310
and might not even be effective
in every given scenario.

00:09:40.310 --> 00:09:42.350
But his teaching
experiments are guided

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:44.690
by strong communication
with his students

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:47.840
and a simple yet
powerful philosophy--

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:49.467
trust the process.

00:09:49.467 --> 00:09:51.800
JEFFREY GROSSMAN: You don't
have to have everything work

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:52.880
perfectly.

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:56.060
But work on it together
with your students.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:59.880
So the goody bags, I was
very excited about them.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:03.350
But I think they really
needed to be tried for a year

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:05.270
and then to figure out
well, OK, what worked

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:06.500
and what didn't work?

00:10:06.500 --> 00:10:07.880
And let me get feedback.

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:09.530
And let me talk to
students about it.

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:11.880
And then let's make them better.

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:13.700
And I think that's
true every year.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:15.260
I always want to teach better.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:18.890
And I always want to
get feedback and realize

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:22.730
what doesn't work and what
does and then amplify what does

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:23.840
and add to it.

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:27.110
And trying new ideas out
is a part of that process.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:30.080
I tell my students all
the time that success--

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:31.220
this is so important.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:35.330
Progress has nothing at all
to do with success, nothing.

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:38.270
Progress has to do
with what we choose

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.490
to do with failure
because that is

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:42.998
then what leads to the success.

00:10:42.998 --> 00:10:44.540
And I try to convey
that to students.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:46.190
And I have to
convey it to myself

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:49.490
as well, that you have to
remind ourselves that we're

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:51.005
all in that same position.

00:10:53.905 --> 00:10:55.530
SARAH HANSEN: And so
Professor Grossman

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:58.020
continues to work on
inspiring his students

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:01.170
to explore new ways of
looking at the world one goody

00:11:01.170 --> 00:11:02.760
bag at a time.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.500
He's made all of his
teaching materials

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.750
from Introduction to
Solid State Chemistry

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:10.890
openly available to you on our
MIT OpenCourseWare website.

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:14.830
So be sure to check them out.

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:16.425
Thank you for listening.

00:11:16.425 --> 00:11:17.800
We hope you're
feeling as excited

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.050
as we are to keep learning.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:22.990
We'd love to hear how you
use the resources on MIT

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:25.990
OpenCourseWare to power change
in your life, classroom,

00:11:25.990 --> 00:11:27.550
or community.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.970
You can send us an email or even
call us to tell us your story.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:32.800
And we'll share it
with other listeners

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:35.050
so they can feel inspired too.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:41.140
Our phone number
is 617-475-0534.

00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:43.690
And you can also find
it in the show notes.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.060
Signing off from
Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:48.940
I'm Sarah Hansen from
MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:51.415
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.850
